                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "\n## ⭐ Rating: 7/10\n\n## ⏱️ Playtime: **21 hours**\n\n**Cyber Warrior** is a weird little mix of hacking puzzle game, escape room, and fake operating system simulator. It feels like Windows 98 had a paranoid breakdown and decided to become a cyber-detective thriller. 💻🕵️‍♂️\n\nThe core idea is genuinely cool. You sit at a computer, receive assignments, break into systems, recover files, decode passwords, inspect fake desktops, read suspicious emails, and slowly uncover the whole Dark Omega conspiracy. When the puzzles click, they feel really satisfying. Some of the best moments are things like T9 phone decoding, hidden letters, file recovery puzzles, image layering, weird password rules, and classic “wait… OH, that’s what it means” moments. 🧩✨\n\nThe atmosphere is easily the strongest part. The fake OS, Remote Control, corrupted files, strange apps, broken fonts, suspicious chats, and creepy instructions like “turn off your firewall, uninstall antivirus, change your password” create a great cyber-paranoia vibe. It really does feel like digging through someone’s unstable machine while something bigger is watching you. 👁️⚠️\n\nBut the game also has one big problem: sometimes it is hard to tell the difference between a clever puzzle, a bug, intentional trolling, and pure UI chaos. 😅 Sometimes you are solving a smart clue. Other times you are staring at 80 random folders wondering whether the developer designed a puzzle or just sneezed into the level editor.\n\nSome puzzles are creative and fun. Others turn into pixel-hunting boss fights where the real enemy is not Dark Omega, but your own patience. The password minigames are funny at first — a great parody of absurd security rules — but after the fifth new condition, it starts to feel like you are fighting the HR department of a bank instead of hackers. 🔐😂\n\nStill, I respect the game a lot. It has personality. It is messy, strange, creative, annoying, clever, and sometimes genuinely brilliant. Not everything works smoothly, but it has a very specific identity, and that matters. In a world full of polished but soulless games, **Cyber Warrior at least feels alive** — even if that lifeform is probably infected with malware. 🦠💾\n\n### Final thoughts\n\n**Cyber Warrior is worth playing if you enjoy fake OS games, hacking aesthetics, cryptic puzzles, and a little bit of suffering as a side dish.**\n\nIt is not always clear. It is not always fair. But when it works, it really works.\n\n**Recommended**, but only if you enjoy games where after solving a puzzle you don’t say:\n“Ah yes, that was obvious.”\n\nYou say:\n“Okay… so that’s the kind of sick brain that designed this.” 😄\n",
